WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:07.290
Sure, it's nice to see actors starring in
roles they were almost destined to play; Robert

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:11.570
Downey Jr. as Tony Stark. Sigourney Weaver
as Ellen Ripley. Arnold Schwarzenegger as

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:16.480
the Terminator. Sylvester Stallone as Rocky.
But sometimes, an actor only has a shot at

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.550
an iconic role after someone else turns it
down. Although everything worked out the way

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:24.099
it was supposed to in the end, we still can't
help wondering what could have been if these

00:00:24.099 --> 00:00:26.339
actors didn’t say no.

00:00:26.339 --> 00:00:29.099
"No! No! No!"

00:00:29.869 --> 00:00:31.599
Thomas Jane as Don Draper

00:00:31.599 --> 00:00:36.140
When we think of Mad Men, we think of the
dashing and duplicitous Jon Hamm. But Hamm

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:40.910
told Marc Maron on the WTF Podcast back in
2011 that the role which gave him his break

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:42.350
almost went to someone else.

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:49.359
"They went to Thomas Jane for it, and they
were told that Thomas Jane does not do television."

00:00:49.359 --> 00:00:53.190
Whether or not that's true, Jane later did
take a regular television role, starring on

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:56.499
HBO's Hung for three seasons.

00:00:56.499 --> 00:00:59.600
Matthew Broderick and John Cusack as Walter
White

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.539
After earning sitcom fame in Malcolm in the
Middle, Bryan Cranston made a huge career

00:01:03.539 --> 00:01:08.700
leap when he was cast as science teacher-turned-meth
kingpin Walter White on Breaking Bad. While

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:12.550
show creator Vince Gilligan always wanted
Cranston, The Hollywood Reporter revealed

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:17.220
that AMC wasn’t interested. Instead, the
network wanted Matthew Broderick or John Cusack

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:22.230
to take on the lead role. Luckily for Cranston,
both babyfaced actors passed, and after execs

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:26.290
saw Cranston’s dramatic work in The X-Files,
everyone was on board.

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:28.420
Or else.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:30.680
Emily Blunt as Black Widow

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:35.220
Scarlett Johansson totally and literally kills
as the Marvel Cinematic Universe version of

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:40.270
Natasha Romanoff, a.k.a. The Black Widow.
But the role was almost given to Emily Blunt,

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:45.610
who had to back out. She's fine with it, though.
As she explained to ScreenRant, "I didn't

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:49.270
do it at the time because it wasn't the right
time and I was contracted to do something

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:53.850
else. But Scarlett has done such a phenomenal
job – she's it!"

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.950
Annette Bening as Catwoman

00:01:55.950 --> 00:02:00.880
For many, Michelle Pfeiffer is the only Catwoman,
having stolen the show in Tim Burton's Batman

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:05.470
Returns. But, as Blastr points out, the role
was originally intended for Annette Bening,

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:09.800
who dropped out when she became pregnant with
her first child right before shooting started.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.620
Pfeiffer had been gunning for the role from
the moment she’d read the script, so she

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.170
slipped right in.

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:16.310
Will Smith as Neo

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:22.190
It's true: Will Smith turned down the iconic
sci-fi action flick The Matrix. Everything's

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:25.410
turned out just fine for the Fresh Prince
since then, but he explained his decision

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:30.970
to Wired in 2004: "The Matrix is a difficult
concept to pitch. In the pitch, I just didn't

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:35.260
see it. I watched Keanu's performance—and
very rarely do I say this—but I would have

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:39.500
messed it up. I would have absolutely messed
up The Matrix." Instead, he saved that honor

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:42.230
for Wild Wild West.

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:43.660
Dougray Scott as Wolverine

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:48.810
Originally, Dougray Scott was tapped to play
Wolverine in the original 2000 film X-Men.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:52.990
But after scheduling conflicts with his role
in Mission: Impossible II, the gig went to

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:56.840
Hugh Jackman, now one of the biggest stars
in the world. No hard feelings, though. Scott

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:01.650
told the Daily Record: "I like Hugh, he's
a really lovely guy. It wasn't as if he stole

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:05.180
it off me. Basically, my movie ran over and
I had to pull out."

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:07.540
Speaking of Jackman...

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:10.000
Hugh Jackman as James Bond

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.069
With Dougray Scott, we had someone passing
on Wolverine to take on the role of a spy.

00:03:14.069 --> 00:03:18.550
Here, we have Wolverine passing on the role
of the most iconic spy in the world. Entertainment

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:22.380
Weekly reports that Jackman was considered
for 007 back when he was filming the second

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:26.750
X-Men film. Jackman would have replaced Pierce
Brosnan after Die Another Day, but Jackman

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:30.610
didn’t feel that it was the right time.
Still, he remarked that it was a very hard

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:36.810
role to pass up. Comic geeks kept their Wolverine,
and the rest of the world got Daniel Craig.

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:39.170
Tom Hanks as Jerry Maguire

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:43.720
Writer and director Cameron Crowe originally
wrote Jerry Maguire with Tom Hanks in mind.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:47.520
Unfortunately, Hanks was a little too old
to play the young, upbeat sports agent that

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:52.590
Tom Cruise made famous. Hanks declined the
project after reading an early draft of the

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:57.660
script. Speaking with Empire Magazine in April
of 1997, Crowe said Hanks passed because he

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:03.480
didn't like the film's romantic subplot between
Maguire and Renee Zellweger's Dorothy Boyd.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:06.209
Pamela Anderson as Dana Scully

00:04:06.209 --> 00:04:10.410
Before agents Mulder and Scully started searching
for aliens in The X-Files, Fox approached

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:14.849
Baywatch star Pamela Anderson to play the
female FBI agent. Entertainment Weekly reported

00:04:14.849 --> 00:04:19.459
that Gillian Anderson was the one who revealed
Fox's original choice for the character. "She

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:22.719
was someone who was more familiar to them
in terms of what was on TV at the time. They

00:04:22.719 --> 00:04:27.610
were looking for someone bustier, taller,
leggier than me." Luckily, Pam passed on the

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:31.889
role and Gillian's intelligent-but-skeptical
demeanor helped provide a great contrast to

00:04:31.889 --> 00:04:34.800
David Duchovny's Mulder, leading the show
to 62 Emmy nominations.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:36.300
And finally...

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:40.979
Jean-Claude Van Damme as the Predator

00:04:40.979 --> 00:04:44.849
Believe it or not, the muscles from Brussels
was originally supposed to play the alien

00:04:44.849 --> 00:04:49.909
hunter in the 1987 classic, Predator. Jean-Claude
Van Damme arrived on set thinking that he

00:04:49.909 --> 00:04:53.409
was going to use his martial arts skills to
fight a bunch of soldiers. It wasn’t too

00:04:53.409 --> 00:04:57.449
long after that when he found out he'd be
wearing a giant, silly monster suit instead.

00:04:57.449 --> 00:05:01.439
Van Damme ran around in the red, rubber suit
for a while, but he hated the role. After

00:05:01.439 --> 00:05:05.439
all, it was a hot suit, and the movie was
filmed in a Mexican jungle. And he thought

00:05:05.439 --> 00:05:06.819
he’d be playing a human.

00:05:06.819 --> 00:05:10.259
Eventually, Van Damme volunteered to body
double for Arnold Schwarzenegger in a few

00:05:10.259 --> 00:05:14.460
of the movie's explosive stunts instead. The
Predator suit was later redesigned for Kevin

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:18.919
Peter Hall, whose near 7-foot-tall height
made him tower over his co-stars, and was

00:05:18.919 --> 00:05:22.020
only slightly more intimidating than Hall’s
other most notable role.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:25.409
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:05:25.409 --> 00:05:28.539
you just saw. And leave us a comment to let
us know which star you’d most like to see

00:05:28.539 --> 00:05:29.039
in the role they’ve turned down...

